18:T11e9,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":["Article","BlogPosting"],"@id":"https://kota.co.uk/blog/the-first-90-days-a-marketing-leaders-guide-to-auditing-the-brand-you-just-inherited/#article","isPartOf":{"@id":"https://kota.co.uk/blog/the-first-90-days-a-marketing-leaders-guide-to-auditing-the-brand-you-just-inherited/"},"author":{"name":"KOTA","@id":"https://kota.co.uk/#/schema/person/0920247d2c12a3b934d2bf67a73d97a2"},"headline":"The first 90 days: a marketing leader&#8217;s guide to auditing the brand you just inherited","datePublished":"2026-09-16T13:57:51+00:00","dateModified":"2026-09-21T15:09:28+00:00","mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://kota.co.uk/blog/the-first-90-days-a-marketing-leaders-guide-to-auditing-the-brand-you-just-inherited/"},"wordCount":15,"publisher":{"@id":"https://kota.co.uk/#organization"},"image":{"@id":"https://kota.co.uk/blog/the-first-90-days-a-marketing-leaders-guide-to-auditing-the-brand-you-just-inherited/#primaryimage"},"thumbnailUrl":"https://kota.co.uk/app/uploads/2026/09/marketers-guide.png","articleSection":["Expertise"],"inLanguage":"en-US"},{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://kota.co.uk/blog/the-first-90-days-a-marketing-leaders-guide-to-auditing-the-brand-you-just-inherited/","url":"https://kota.co.uk/blog/the-first-90-days-a-marketing-leaders-guide-to-auditing-the-brand-you-just-inherited/","name":"The first 90 days: a marketing leader's guide to auditing the brand you just inherited - KOTA","isPartOf":{"@id":"https://kota.co.uk/#website"},"primaryImageOfPage":{"@id":"https://kota.co.uk/blog/the-first-90-days-a-marketing-leaders-guide-to-auditing-the-brand-you-just-inherited/#primaryimage"},"image":{"@id":"https://kota.co.uk/blog/the-first-90-days-a-marketing-leaders-guide-to-auditing-the-brand-you-just-inherited/#primaryimage"},"thumbnailUrl":"https://kota.co.uk/app/uploads/2026/09/marketers-guide.png","datePublished":"2026-09-16T13:57:51+00:00","dateModified":"2026-09-21T15:09:28+00:00","breadcrumb":{"@id":"https://kota.co.uk/blog/the-first-90-days-a-marketing-leaders-guide-to-auditing-the-brand-you-just-inherited/#breadcrumb"},"inLanguage":"en-US","potentialAction":[{"@type":"ReadAction","target":["https://kota.co.uk/blog/the-first-90-days-a-marketing-leaders-guide-to-auditing-the-brand-you-just-inherited/"]}]},{"@type":"ImageObject","inLanguage":"en-US","@id":"https://kota.co.uk/blog/the-first-90-days-a-marketing-leaders-guide-to-auditing-the-brand-you-just-inherited/#primaryimage","url":"https://kota.co.uk/app/uploads/2026/09/marketers-guide.png","contentUrl":"https://kota.co.uk/app/uploads/2026/09/marketers-guide.png","width":1400,"height":787},{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","@id":"https://kota.co.uk/blog/the-first-90-days-a-marketing-leaders-guide-to-auditing-the-brand-you-just-inherited/#breadcrumb","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://kota.co.uk/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"The first 90 days: a marketing leader&#8217;s guide to auditing the brand you just inherited"}]},{"@type":"WebSite","@id":"https://kota.co.uk/#website","url":"https://kota.co.uk/","name":"KOTA","description":"","publisher":{"@id":"https://kota.co.uk/#organization"},"potentialAction":[{"@type":"SearchAction","target":{"@type":"EntryPoint","urlTemplate":"https://kota.co.uk/?s={search_term_string}"},"query-input":{"@type":"PropertyValueSpecification","valueRequired":true,"valueName":"search_term_string"}}],"inLanguage":"en-US"},{"@type":"Organization","@id":"https://kota.co.uk/#organization","name":"KOTA","alternateName":"KOTA creative","url":"https://kota.co.uk/","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","inLanguage":"en-US","@id":"https://kota.co.uk/#/schema/logo/image/","url":"https://kota.co.uk/app/uploads/2024/11/Logo-Profile.png","contentUrl":"https://kota.co.uk/app/uploads/2024/11/Logo-Profile.png","width":1080,"height":1080,"caption":"KOTA"},"image":{"@id":"https://kota.co.uk/#/schema/logo/image/"},"sameAs":["https://www.facebook.com/kotacreative/","https://x.com/kotacreative"]},{"@type":"Person","@id":"https://kota.co.uk/#/schema/person/0920247d2c12a3b934d2bf67a73d97a2","name":"KOTA","image":{"@type":"ImageObject","inLanguage":"en-US","@id":"https://kota.co.uk/#/schema/person/image/","url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/997a25aeeb59c30e7b958976b63b4e9a84a8addc4e811d9c09e6a924a059c98e?s=96&d=mm&r=g","contentUrl":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/997a25aeeb59c30e7b958976b63b4e9a84a8addc4e811d9c09e6a924a059c98e?s=96&d=mm&r=g","caption":"KOTA"},"sameAs":["https://kota.co.uk/wp"],"url":"https://kota.co.uk/author/kota/"}]}1e:T1d9f,<p>You&#8217;ve got the job. Congratulations! Now open your new work&#8217;s website&#8230;</p>
<p>If something in you winces, you&#8217;re not imagining it. And you&#8217;re definitely not alone. A huge number of marketing leaders start a new role and, within the first few weeks, quietly diagnose the exact same problem: the brand doesn&#8217;t match the business any more. Not because anyone did anything wrong. Just because the company kept moving and nobody circled back to update the story.</p>
<p>Believe me, this happens so often it&#8217;s basically a rite of passage. You inherit a logo, a site, a tone of voice, a pile of old brand guidelines nobody&#8217;s opened since 2021—possibly stored in a PDF called &#8220;FINAL_v3_USE_THIS_ONE,&#8221; possibly guarded by a single ex-employee who still hasn&#8217;t told you where the original logo files live. And you&#8217;re expected to have an opinion on all of it by week two, ideally a diplomatic one.</p>
<p>Here&#8217;s the thing nobody tells you: not everything that feels wrong actually is wrong. Some of it might be genuinely broken. Some of might just feel uncomfortable because it&#8217;s new to you, not because it&#8217;s failing the business. Knowing the difference is the whole job in those first 90 days.</p>
<h2>Start with what the brand is actually doing, not how it makes you feel</h2>
<p>New leaders often start with taste. Wrong colour, dated typeface, that stock photo of people inexplicably laughing at a laptop (why??). I know, I know, bad taste is annoying to look at every day. But taste isn&#8217;t strategy, and a brand refresh built on personal preference is a hard thing to defend to your CEO in month four.</p>
<p>Instead, start with performance. Is the site converting? Are people finding the case studies? Does the messaging match what sales are actually saying on calls? Pull the analytics before you pull the mood board. If the bounce rate is fine and the pipeline&#8217;s healthy, you might be looking at a brand that&#8217;s ugly but working. And &#8220;ugly but working&#8221; is a much easier problem to sit with for a while than &#8220;broken and I inherited it.&#8221;</p>
<h2>Look for the gap between what the business is now and what the brand still says</h2>
<p>This is the pattern that shows up again and again, across every sector: the business changes faster than the way it describes itself. A company doubles in size and the website still reads like a five-person outfit operating out of someone&#8217;s spare room. A business repositions from a narrow service into a broader platform, and the homepage hasn&#8217;t caught up to a decision that was made a year ago internally.</p>
<p>Go through the site with one blunt question in mind: does this describe the company as it exists today, or the company as it existed when this was last written? If you&#8217;re finding yourself explaining the gap out loud to prospects (&#8220;we&#8217;ve actually moved beyond that now&#8221;) that&#8217;s not a taste problem. That&#8217;s a brand that&#8217;s lying to people by accident.</p>
<h2>Check who the brand is actually built for</h2>
<p>Most B2B websites are quietly written for one person, even when the business is selling to several. A procurement lead and a design lead don&#8217;t want the same proof points. A finance stakeholder and the person who&#8217;ll actually use the product every day aren&#8217;t persuaded by the same page. If your site reads like it was written for a single buyer and you&#8217;re selling to four, that&#8217;s definitely worth flagging early. It&#8217;s a strategic gap, not a cosmetic one, and it&#8217;s usually invisible to people who&#8217;ve stopped noticing it.</p>
<h2>Take the case studies seriously, they&#8217;ll tell you more than the homepage will</h2>
<p>If you want the fastest read on how healthy a brand actually is, skip the homepage and go straight to the proof of work. Are the case studies easy to find? Do they show real outcomes, or just nice photography and a client logo? For most B2B buyers who don&#8217;t already know your business, this is the page doing the actual persuading. If it&#8217;s three clicks deep, thin, or missing entirely, you&#8217;ve found something worth fixing that has nothing to do with colour palettes.</p>
<h2>Ask what the team can actually change without a developer</h2>
<p>This one rarely makes it onto a brand audit, but it should. If updating a single page, publishing a blog post or adding a new case study means filing a ticket and waiting a fortnight, the brand isn&#8217;t really yours to run, it belongs to whoever built the CMS. Test this in week one. Try to make one small change yourself. What you learn about how much friction is baked into the system will tell you more about your next 90 days than any brand workshop will.</p>
<h2>Don&#8217;t rush to call it a rebrand</h2>
<p>Most of us marketing leaders assume the fix is a full rebrand, because that&#8217;s the version of the job that feels most visible and most ours. It rarely is. Most brands that feel broken aren&#8217;t wrong at their core, they&#8217;re just under-expressed. The logo, the colours, the fundamental idea of the business are usually fine. What&#8217;s actually gone stale is how sharply that idea comes through in the work.</p>
<p>Protecting what already exists and sharpening how it&#8217;s expressed is a much easier sell internally than &#8220;let&#8217;s start from zero,&#8221; and it&#8217;s usually the right call anyway. Evolution over revolution isn&#8217;t a compromise. It&#8217;s what actually works.</p>
<h2>The audit is the easy part. Knowing what to prioritise is the job.</h2>
<p>By the end of your first 90 days, you&#8217;ll probably have a list — messy hierarchy, outdated positioning, a CMS that fights you, a case studies section that&#8217;s an afterthought, a homepage built for the wrong buyer. Don&#8217;t try to fix all of it in the first quarter. Pick the thing that&#8217;s actually costing the business something, not the thing that&#8217;s bothering you personally the most.</p>
<p>And if the list feels bigger than you expected, that&#8217;s not a sign you&#8217;ve failed the audit. It&#8217;s a sign you did it properly.</p>
<p>You don&#8217;t have to work out what to do with it on your own, either. This is genuinely the bit we&#8217;re best at: coming in after the audit, helping you figure out what&#8217;s worth fixing first, and doing the work without making you start again from scratch. <a href="https://kota.co.uk/contact/">If you want a second pair of eyes on that list, we&#8217;re happy to take a look.</a></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p data-start="2127" data-end="2244" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Looking for a <a href="https://kota.co.uk/service/brand-strategy-and-identity">London branding agency</a>?</p>
<h3><strong>About KOTA</strong></h3>
<p>KOTA is a <a href="https://kota.co.uk/">global branding agency and creative web design studio based in london</a>. Our brand-to-build approach brings <a href="https://kota.co.uk/service/brand-strategy">strategy</a>, <a href="https://kota.co.uk/service/brand-identity-design">identity</a>, <a href="https://kota.co.uk/service/creative-web-design/">creative web design</a>, <a href="https://kota.co.uk/service/web-development/">development</a> and <a href="https://kota.co.uk/service/growth-marketing/">growth marketing</a> into one connected process. We help ambitious businesses find brand clarity, turn it into creatively confident work and build digital systems that create commercial momentum.</p>
